    "question": {
      ":question/id": "d9476811-88e9-534e-85e5-9e243f2e959a",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}_{1} &=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\qquad \\mathbf{a}_{2} &=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\mathbf{a}_{3} &=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind a basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $S=\\operatorname{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_{1},\\mathbf{a}_{2},\\mathbf{a}_{3}\\}$ by identifying pivot columns.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Place the vectors in the columns of a matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nApply $R_{2}\\leftarrow R_{2}-R_{1}$:\n\n$$\nA\\sim \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe leading nonzero entries are in columns $1$. Select the corresponding columns from the **original matrix**, giving $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{a}_{1}\\}$:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThese pivot columns are linearly independent and span the column space of $A$, which is $S$. The nonpivot columns can be removed without changing the span. Row operations locate the pivot indices; use the original vectors when writing the basis.",
